{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2724 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "07f73b6c122f53efbbd0905821de47224e619cc1fd7521ffa39192245aaf7312" :edb/db-before-t 2723 :edb/db-after-t 2724 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536036 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:22:08.032Z" 13194139536036 true] [17592186166826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380405 13194139536036 true] [17592186166826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380412 13194139536036 true] [17592186166826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380419 13194139536036 true] [17592186166827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380426 13194139536036 true] [17592186166997 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536036 true] [17592186166997 1100 "The second-order differential equation $y^{″} - 2y^{′} + y = 0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where\n\n$x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}, y(t) = x_{1}(t), y^{′}(t) = x_{2}(t)$.\nFind the matrix $A$." 13194139536036 false] [17592186166997 1100 "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186166997 1145 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}-y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380405 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380406 13194139536036 true] [17592186380405 1089 "q-109635" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536036 true] [17592186380405 1100 "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}-16y=0,\\,y(1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380405 1113 #uuid "b332ac9d-e1aa-5efa-b417-32a7d962209b" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380405 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=8{y}^{′}+16y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=8{x}_{2}+16{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=3,q=1,f(t)=16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q)\\cdot f(t) & =(3-1)(16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =2(8)(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380407 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380408 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380409 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380410 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380411 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1041 "selection" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380410 13194139536036 true] [17592186380406 1110 #uuid "725311ef-1f3d-5db4-adca-0f73a6abd290" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380407 1034 "8(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380407 1111 #uuid "dd0d3599-6297-59ce-8a64-c99e931a4330" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380408 1034 "4(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380408 1111 #uuid "adc5b24c-fc24-5d47-b284-477597052b8b" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380409 1034 "6({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380409 1111 #uuid "5fee1643-0534-59f5-a028-0f614a9bbcda" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380410 1034 "16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380410 1111 #uuid "c1ad7f3e-4506-5467-b6a0-c04078ff1bab" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380411 1034 "16({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380411 1111 #uuid "6b4c0797-21eb-5f1e-9ea3-8f99dee48abb" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380412 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380413 13194139536036 true] [17592186380412 1089 "q-109636" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536036 true] [17592186380412 1100 "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,y=0,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380412 1113 #uuid "14da67e4-ca85-5189-99d0-c71c3051d763" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380412 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=10{y}^{′}-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=10{x}_{2}-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-1,q=2,f(t)=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(-1-2)(-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3(-5)(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380414 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380415 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380416 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380417 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380418 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1041 "selection" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380418 13194139536036 true] [17592186380413 1110 #uuid "9497cbb4-0690-57c4-8ade-12f260987f50" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380414 1034 "-12(3{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380414 1111 #uuid "771c8137-7dd9-5758-bb0a-ffc6dc52d469" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380415 1034 "-15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380415 1111 #uuid "82b63a8a-38e3-5ba5-bd8a-89d58d436f7a" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380416 1034 "12(3{x}_{1}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380416 1111 #uuid "bf6d2c00-369a-5efc-b831-acbe6746bb70" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380417 1034 "10(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380417 1111 #uuid "839f1a25-3851-5a20-af1e-3a5c6d4af041" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380418 1034 "15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536036 true] [17592186380418 1111 #uuid "a35bcfa7-f0d1-5df7-a71d-27c1b0711d93" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380419 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380420 13194139536036 true] [17592186380419 1089 "q-109644" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380419 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536036 true] [17592186380419 1100 "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=4\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q-r)\\cdot f(t).$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380419 1113 #uuid "4d721395-7f60-58c6-8c94-e984d75af312" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380419 1145 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(0) & ={x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{y}^{″}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{x}_{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=-1,r=4,f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-(-1)-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(2+1-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(-1)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536036 true] [17592186380420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380421 13194139536036 true] [17592186380420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380422 13194139536036 true] [17592186380420 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380423 13194139536036 true] [17